NAME
    ls - plain directory listing

SYNOPSIS
    ls [path]

DESCRIPTION
    Lists a directory (the current one with no path) in the order its
    entries are stored, one line each: the name, a space, then <DIR>
    for a directory or the size in bytes for a file, and "  @AAAA" when
    the file has a load address. The last line is the count
    ("7 entries"). Names starting with "." are left out, "." and ".."
    among them. ls is /BIN/LS.

    Errors: "ls: not found", "ls: not a directory" (a file was named).

    ls is a small, direct user of the file API (opendir, readdir and
    the ent_* accessors, see fs); dir is the full lister: sorted,
    globs, -R and -S.

LIMITS
    Sizes are the low 16 bits: a file of 64K or more shows its size
    modulo 65536.

EXAMPLES
    ls
    ls /BIN

NOTES
    New in Y1/OS 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    dir, tree, fs
